resources.string:
· Sleep = suspend to RAM: wakes almost instantly, but network services stop — this tab shows offline and pages won\'t load (expected). Wake it with “Wake” (needs MAC + WoL);
· Shut down: powers off at once; web service stops — afterwards only “Wake” (needs MAC) or the physical button;
· Restart: reboots at once; the web session drops — heartbeat returns online automatically after reboot;
· Wake: sends a magic packet to power on; needs MAC and WoL enabled in BIOS.

· Wake route configured → send to the route\'s address/port (select specific MACs);
· SSH jump host configured → it broadcasts on the intranet (works from outside);
· Neither → the phone broadcasts on the LAN (phone must be on the same subnet);
· Only “remote access” available (public tunnel while away) → wake can\'t be sent: a magic packet is a LAN UDP broadcast and can\'t cross a tunnel. Restart/shutdown/sleep call the web API, which works over the tunnel.

① Wake on LAN enabled in BIOS (affects wake only);
② Using the wired NIC\'s MAC — most wireless NICs don\'t support wake-from-off;
③ MAC entered on the matching address row in “Edit server” (e.g. 192.168.0.120:12300 AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF);
④ Broadcast address/port in the route correct (matching sol\'s listener);
⑤ Target and phone on the same subnet (LAN broadcast only works within it).

① Wake on LAN enabled in BIOS (affects wake only);
② Using the wired NIC\'s MAC — most wireless NICs don\'t support wake-from-off;
③ MAC on the matching address row in “Edit server” (e.g. 192.168.0.120:12300 AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF);
④ Broadcast address/port in the route correct (matching sol\'s listener);
⑤ Host isn\'t unplugged and the power strip isn\'t off (the NIC needs standby power to receive packets when off).
